The corpus data that the Glint site publishes, and that `glint corpus update` downloads, contains third-party text:

  * ASN.1 modules extracted from RFCs and Internet-Drafts. They are code components under the IETF Trust's Revised BSD
    License (Trust Legal Provisions; BCP 78 and BCP 79). Each module record names the RFC and section it
    comes from (for a draft, its revision).

  * ASN.1 modules from ITU-T Recommendations (for example X.501, X.509 and X.520), which are the property
    of ITU-T and are included with credit to ITU-T. Each record keeps its Recommendation number and the
    URL it was taken from.

  * ASN.1 modules from ISO and ISO/IEC International Standards (ISO 8571-4, ISO/IEC 9541-2 and
    ISO/IEC 10031-2), as ITU-T publishes them on its formal-language site, which are the property of ISO
    and IEC and are included with credit to ISO and IEC. Each record keeps its standard's number and the
    URL it was taken from.

Standalone modules are bundled as published, comments included, so each keeps the copyright notice and
disclaimer it carries (for an ISO/IEC module, "Copyright (c) ISO/IEC <year>. This version of this ASN.1 module
is part of ISO/IEC <standard>; see the ISO|IEC text itself for full legal notices.").

The same notices are part of every build of the data (`info.notices` of `corpus.json`).
